o feed management  implementation  you know we were paying  about 2800 per group per year and  essentially you could do up to five  groups  uh for up to three years you had your  choice you could do one group for one  year or you could do five groups for  three years  so yeah as you can see it was uh you  know pretty enticing um  amount of financial assistance to do  that obviously our our under our hu  stands for historically underserved  producers so those  are our  limited and beginning farmers and then  our different  diverse  groups of farmers  next slide so again what makes up a feed  management plan we get the benchmark  plan developed by our our plan writer  and then we have a plan for each group  we get the lab analysis for everything  that's being fed  that those labs had to be  so we had manure testing so  the  labs that were testing the manure had to  be to be accredited by penn state  or or the university of pennsylvania and  again our plan had to show what  adjustments were needed and then follow  up on what was actually implemented at  the farm  and again  you know looking for those dietary  adjustments in that plan  next slide and then one other option we  had was our our cap plans our  conservation activity plans they have  now been changed to a different format  now but in the sake of time we won't  really get into that but  if a producer only wanted a benchmark  plan just to see what was going on the  farm but wasn't ready for implementation  they could get what we call this  conservation activity plan and that's  basically a benchmark evaluation so  one thing that we found  as far as nrcs  rules go you have to be  those plans have to be written by a tsp  so unfortunately  we have to date we have not funded any  of those because again we have no tsps  currently to do this so  something that we're we're looking at  hopefully changing in the years to come  but you know not a bad payment just to  get a benchmark evaluation of what's  going on in the 